Package: argus-tty
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, openssh-server, sudo, passwd, bsdutils, e2fsprogs, curl, logrotate, systemd
Suggests: awscli, libpam-google-authenticator
Description: One-click developer/admin/deployer access with full session auditing
 Creates real per-user Linux accounts across three tiers (developer: sudo
 whitelist; admin: full sudo; deployer: narrowest, deploy/restart only),
 provisions their SSH key (or generates one), forces every SSH session
 through a TTY + command-history recorder via an sshd Match block (plus an
 AllowGroups gate that denies login to any account outside these tiers),
 uploads recordings to S3, and emails connect/disconnect notifications
 through a privilege-separated SMTP mailer. Every network call (S3, SMTP)
 is optional and non-blocking: if unconfigured, sessions still record
 locally and nothing ever holds a connection open.
 .
 EMAIL_ENABLED and S3_UPLOAD_ENABLED in /etc/argus-tty/config.env are master
 on/off switches for the two network integrations: EMAIL_ENABLED directly
 controls whether the argus-tty-mailer.timer systemd unit is enabled or
 disabled, and S3_UPLOAD_ENABLED gates the S3 upload logic the same way.
 .
 A pre-existing privileged account (e.g. the cloud-init default 'ubuntu'
 user) can be recorded in "lite" mode instead — command history and session
 metadata, no full keystroke-by-keystroke transcript — via
 LITE_RECORDING_USERS in config.env.
 .
 GOOGLE_2FA_ENABLED in config.env optionally requires a Google Authenticator
 (TOTP) code, in addition to the SSH key, for developer/admin sessions —
 deployer accounts and LITE_RECORDING_USERS are exempt. Off by default.
 .
 Developer/admin/deployer sudo permissions are whitelists you can override
 per host — see /etc/argus-tty/{developer,admin,deployer}.sudoers. The tier
 names themselves are also customizable, via DEVELOPER_GROUP_NAME/
 ADMIN_GROUP_NAME/DEPLOYER_GROUP_NAME in config.env.
 .
 Every account also joins a shared webapps group (and rvm, if installed),
 scaffolding for whatever app-deployment tooling you layer on top of these
 accounts — this package doesn't install or require any specific one.
 .
 Run `sudo argus-tty status` after install to check configuration.
